{"uid":"2926b7bf174c1793","name":"test_an_illegal_reported_status_ends_the_run_as_a_contract_violation","fullName":"tests.test_task_status_lifecycle#test_an_illegal_reported_status_ends_the_run_as_a_contract_violation","historyId":"87cdd44f7380d3d3eee52d77ef90dba7","time":{"start":1791867169329,"stop":1791867169346,"duration":17},"description":"v0.13 task 004 (#92 site 2) strengthens this. The OLD claim, pinned\n    here before the cutover, was just: the FIRST bad payload set\n    `sup._abort_reason` to a \"contract violation\" message and ended the run\n    outright -- a single reported illegal move was fatal on the first\n    offence, with no re-ask and no ladder at all.\n\n    The NEW claim keeps the old one's real content -- the illegal move is\n    still refused, the task record is still untouched -- and adds what the\n    old assertion could not see: the errors were carried back to the SAME\n    hat verbatim on each re-ask, the ladder ran the FULL\n    `contract_retry_max + 1` attempts (never fewer, never more) through\n    `contract_enforcement.enforce_contract` -- the EXACT SAME retry loop\n    task 010 built for a malformed/absent/wrong-shape payload, per this\n    module's own opening docstring claim -- and the outcome is an\n    agent-fault iteration that \"continued per the table\"\n    (`contract_enforcement`'s own docstring language): here, with the\n    run's own iteration budget already spent by the one charged attempt\n    below (`iterations=1`), \"continuing per the table\" is the ordinary\n    tail, the SAME budget-boundary rule v0.13 task 003 (#92) established\n    for a schema violation -- never a second mechanism.\n    ","descriptionHtml":"<p>v0.13 task 004 (#92 site 2) strengthens this. The OLD claim, pinned<br />\nhere before the cutover, was just: the FIRST bad payload set<br />\n<code>sup._abort_reason</code> to a &quot;contract violation&quot; message and ended the run<br />\noutright -- a single reported illegal move was fatal on the first<br />\noffence, with no re-ask and no ladder at all.</p>\n<pre><code>The NEW claim keeps the old one's real content -- the illegal move is\nstill refused, the task record is still untouched -- and adds what the\nold assertion could not see: the errors were carried back to the SAME\nhat verbatim on each re-ask, the ladder ran the FULL\n`contract_retry_max + 1` attempts (never fewer, never more) through\n`contract_enforcement.enforce_contract` -- the EXACT SAME retry loop\ntask 010 built for a malformed/absent/wrong-shape payload, per this\nmodule's own opening docstring claim -- and the outcome is an\nagent-fault iteration that &quot;continued per the table&quot;\n(`contract_enforcement`'s own docstring language): here, with the\nrun's own iteration budget already spent by the one charged attempt\nbelow (`iterations=1`), &quot;continuing per the table&quot; is the ordinary\ntail, the SAME budget-boundary rule v0.13 task 003 (#92) established\nfor a schema violation -- never a second mechanism.\n</code></pre>\n","status":"passed","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[{"name":"event_loop_policy","time":{"start":1791866910617,"stop":1791866910618,"duration":1},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"tmp_path_factory","time":{"start":1791866910618,"stop":1791866910618,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"monkeypatch","time":{"start":1791867169308,"stop":1791867169309,"duration":1},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"_isolate_ambient_home_in_process",